{
  "rules": [
    "Systematic Underestimation of Bullish Spikes: The model consistently fails to capture the magnitude of rapid price increases (e.g., the jump from ~76 to ~88 in late November), forecasting only a slow, linear drift. Correction: Apply a non-linear upward multiplier when sentiment history shows consecutive high values (>= 0.67) combined with a recent price breakout above the 18-day moving average.",
    "Excessive Lag in Trend Reversal: Forecasts exhibit a significant lag in recognizing trend shifts, continuing to project gradual increases even after the true price has peaked and begun to fall. Correction: Introduce a momentum decay factor that accelerates the forecast slope downward once the 3-day price change turns negative, regardless of the long-term sentiment average.",
    "Failure to Anticipate Mean Reversion: The model assumes a persistent upward trajectory, ignoring the strong mean-reverting behavior observed after the peak (dropping from ~89 to ~77). Correction: Implement a mean-reversion heuristic that pulls the forecast toward the 18-day historical mean when the forecasted price exceeds the historical high by more than 5%.",
    "Horizon-Specific Bias: Errors are minimal for the first 1-2 days but grow exponentially over the 30-day horizon. Correction: Scale the forecast correction factor inversely with the forecast horizon; apply stronger adjustments for day 15-30 predictions compared to day 1-5.",
    "Sentiment-Price Decoupling: High sentiment scores (1.00) do not trigger sufficient price adjustments in the model's linear logic. Correction: Weight the sentiment input non-linearly (e.g., square the sentiment score) to amplify the impact of strong bullish signals on the forecast trajectory."
  ]
}
